    body:"<p>The capture curve is the argument. Inspecting the same 8% of units \u2014 but choosing <em>which</em> 8% by predicted risk rather than at random \u2014 catches several times more defects for identical inspection effort. Nothing about the plant changes except the order of the queue.</p><p>The cost curve then answers the manager's second question. Missed defects cost far more than inspection minutes, so the total-cost minimum sits well above the current 8%. Present the table and let operations pick the row: the model supplies the capture rate at each level, the business supplies the cost of a return.</p><p><strong>The more valuable finding is in the feature importance.</strong> If one machine-shift combination carries a disproportionate share of defects, the cheapest intervention is not more inspection at all \u2014 it is maintenance or recalibration on that line. Inspection catches defects; fixing the machine stops producing them. Always report both, and lead with the second.</p>",
